course: dictionary
lesson: 1162
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Bristle can be a thing you can touch, or something an animal does, or even something a person does. Read the words around it to find out which meaning fits.
explain: «A worm's body is made of many rings, and tiny bristles on them grip the soil.» Here bristles are the short, stiff hairs that help the worm hold on. Try your meaning in the sentence: does "tiny stiff hairs grip the soil" make sense? Yes, so you are right.
explain: «A cat's fur bristles when it feels threatened.» Bristles is a verb here. The cat's hair stands up stiffly because it is scared or angry, which is a warning to keep away. A person can bristle too: they suddenly show they are annoyed.
fact: Bristle can be a noun (a bristle, bristles) or a verb (bristle, bristling, bristled).
fact: A bristle is a short, stiff hair, like the hairs on a toothbrush, a hairbrush or a pig's back.
fact: «Bristles» can mean the hairs of a brush: «A toothbrush with bent, splayed bristles does not clean well.»
fact: When an animal's fur bristles, it stands up stiffly because the animal is scared or angry.
fact: «Bristle with» means full of or covered with many things, as in «the shelf bristles with books».
exercise: «A toothbrush with bent, splayed bristles does not clean well.» What are the bristles here? | A type of toothpaste | Bones inside a worm | Soft bread crumbs | *Short, stiff hairs on the brush
exercise: «The cat's fur bristled when the dog came near.» What does bristled mean? | *Stood up stiffly because it was scared or angry | Went to sleep | Lay flat and smooth | Shone brightly in the sun
exercise: «The shelf bristled with books.» What does bristled with mean? | *Full of, covered with many things | Falling down under the weight | Ready to be read later | Empty of everything
test: «He bristled at her rude remark.» What does bristled at mean? | Ran outside to play | *Was suddenly annoyed by it | Fell asleep in his chair | Laughed loudly at the joke
test: Find and copy the word that means the stiff hairs on a brush: «Loose brushwork, holding the brush further from the bristles, often makes paintings look more alive.» | paintings | *bristles | alive | brushwork
exam: Which clue helps you choose the right meaning of bristle in «The worm's tiny bristles grip the soil»? | The colour of the page | *The words around it, such as worm, grip and soil | Whether the word starts with a capital letter | The number of letters in the word
